# PulseNSFW v2 — env placeholders. Do not commit real values.

## Supabase reader accounts (round 2D)

Auth choice: email magic link (one-time link, no password). The anon key is
safe in the browser bundle; the service-role key is server-only and bypasses
RLS — use it only in server code, always scoped to the signed-in user_id.

# PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<anon key>
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service role key, server-side only>

Migrations live in supabase/migrations/ (apply with the Supabase CLI):
- supabase/migrations/0001_reader_profiles.sql

## Age verification vendor (round 2D)

Empty both = built-in stub stays (local `npm run dev` needs no vendor).
Set BOTH to switch the same gate UI to a real third-party verifier. The
vendor hosts any document/selfie/card flow; this site only posts a token.

# AGE_VENDOR_API_URL=https://<vendor>/v1/verify
# AGE_VENDOR_API_KEY=<server bearer key from the verifier>

## Amazon SES mail (round 2D)

Empty region/keys/from = subscribe and contact forms report "not configured"
honestly. With values set, both send plain-text email only — no HTML blasts.
MAIL_FROM should be a verified SES identity, e.g. "PulseNSFW" <news@pulsensfw.com>.

# MAIL_REGION=us-east-1
# MAIL_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<iam access key id, server-only>
# MAIL_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<iam secret access key, server-only>
# MAIL_FROM="PulseNSFW" <news@pulsensfw.com>
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=<optional reply-to, usually the list inbox>
# NEWS_TO=<desk inbox for contact mail plumbed through the same helper>

## Drafts-only and publish keys for the agent API must also stay server-side.
